00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.260
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.940
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Anna Roth from the

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.540
Microsoft Cognitive Services

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.320
Computer Vision Team.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.320
Today I'm here talking about our

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.460
custom vision service.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.340
So the custom vision service,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.240
which is available at

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.420
customvision.ai is a service for

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.200
helping people build their own

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.220
custom image classifiers, so

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.660
recognize objects or things that

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.520
they care about.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.840
In this example, I have built a

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.460
classifier to recognize plants

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.920
that are available at the

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.740
Japanese Garden in Seattle.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.960
I want to be able to recognize

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.340
things like a Japanese pine, a

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.800
cypress or a Japanese maple.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.560
The way that you do this is you

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.460
upload some example images, say

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.300
maybe 50 images per class, and

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.720
then all you have to do is hit

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.780
train and you have a classifier

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.780
that's hosted for you at an API,

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.540
at an http endpoint.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.740
So I've already built my

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:52.840
classifier and trained it, and I

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:53.880
have it right here.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:55.300
Now I'm going to run a quick

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.120
test to see if it's working.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.120
So I'm doing a quick test here.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.540
And I'll just grab an image that

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.720
I took earlier, that I had from

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.460
earlier.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.100
It's a picture of a Japanese

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:06.620
maple.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.940
Let's see if we get this right.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.700
So I've uploaded the image here

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.640
And then with very, very high

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.000
confidence, the trends of my

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.720
classifier predicted that it's a

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.280
Japanese maple.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.500
The other neat thing about this

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.280
system is that every image that

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.180
you send for classification is

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:22.800
saved.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.820
So all these images that I

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.740
submitted previously have been

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.580
saved here.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.580
I can actually go label them and

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:28.740
then over time improve my

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.660
classifier.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:30.840
So the custom vision service is

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.300
a really, really easy way for

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.140
anybody to build a classifier in

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.460
a couple of minutes and improve

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.620
it over time.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.740
I hope you'll give it a chance

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.540
to try it out.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.780
You can do so by going to

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.060
customvision.ai.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.500
That's customvision.ai and give

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.640
it a shot today.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.520
Thank you.

